.TH JOURNQL 1 "" "" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
journql \- analyze a systemd Journal Selection with SQL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
journql [OPTIONS] \-\- [JOURNAL SELECTION] \-\- 'JOURNAL QUERY'
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B journql
reads Journal Entries that
.BR journalctl (1)
selects.
It makes them available to a Journal Query through an in-memory DuckDB
database.
The query uses the stable
.B journal
relation and can also read each complete Journal Entry from its
.B entry
JSON column.
.PP
The command has three parts.
The first
.B \-\-
separator ends
.B journql
options.
The second
.B \-\-
separator ends the Journal Selection.
The Journal Query must be exactly one shell argument after the second
separator.
Put the SQL text in single quotes so that the shell supplies it as one
argument.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show command help and exit.
Use this option alone.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the
.B journql
and DuckDB versions and exit.
Use this option alone.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " " FORMAT
.TQ
.BI \-\-format= FORMAT
Select the Query Result format.
.I FORMAT
is
.BR table ,
.BR csv ,
or
.BR json .
The default is
.BR table .
Use this option at most once.
.SH "JOURNAL SELECTION"
If the Journal Selection is empty,
.B journql
uses the normal
.B journalctl
selection.
It does not add a time limit or a size limit.
.B journql
supports the following Journal Selection controls.
.SS Journal source
.BR \-\-system ,
.BR \-\-user ,
and
.BR \-\-merge " (" \-m ")."
.SS Time, cursor, and boot position
.BR \-\-since " (" \-S "),"
.BR \-\-until " (" \-U "),"
.BR \-\-cursor " (" \-c "),"
.BR \-\-after\-cursor ,
and
.BR \-\-boot " (" \-b ")."
.SS Journal fields
.BR \-\-unit " (" \-u "),"
.BR \-\-user\-unit ,
.BR \-\-identifier " (" \-t "),"
.BR \-\-priority " (" \-p "),"
and
.BR \-\-facility .
.SS Text and kernel selection
.BR \-\-grep " (" \-g "),"
.BR \-\-ignore\-case " (" \-i "),"
.BR \-\-case\-sensitive[=BOOLEAN] ,
and
.BR \-\-dmesg " (" \-k ")."
.B journql
passes
.B \-\-ignore\-case
and
.B \-i
to
.B journalctl
as
.BR \-\-case\-sensitive=no .
.SS Number and order
.BR \-\-lines " (" \-n ")"
and
.BR \-\-reverse " (" \-r ")."
.SS Native field matches
.B journql
supports Journal field matches such as
.B MESSAGE=failed
and the
.B +
disjunction operator.
It does not support path matches.
.PP
Options that need a value accept a separate argument.
.B journql
also supports the
.BI \-\-option= value
form where applicable.
You can omit the optional values for
.BR \-\-boot ,
.BR \-\-lines ,
and
.BR \-\-case\-sensitive .
.B journql
supports attached values for documented short forms that take a value.
.PP
.B journql
controls the journal data format, complete field values, and pager behavior.
It rejects other
.B journalctl
arguments, including output controls, paging, follow mode, cursor files,
remote or alternate journal sources, and maintenance commands.
The command runs
.B journalctl
with the authority of the current user.
.SH "JOURNAL QUERY"
The Journal Query is SQL that DuckDB runs against an in-memory database.
It must be exactly one shell argument, but it can contain one or more SQL
statements.
.B journql
does not rewrite the Query Result.
.B journql
writes only the Query Result to standard output.
It writes diagnostics from
.BR journql ,
.BR journalctl ,
and DuckDB to standard error.
.SH "RESULT FORMATS"
.TP
.B table
Write a table Query Result.
This is the default.
.TP
.B csv
Write a CSV Query Result with column names.
.TP
.B json
Write a JSON Query Result.
.SH "JOURNAL RELATION"
The
.B journal
relation has the following stable columns, including when the Journal
Selection is empty.
.TS
tab(|) allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Column|SQL type|Journal Entry field
timestamp|TIMESTAMPTZ|__REALTIME_TIMESTAMP
message|VARCHAR|MESSAGE
hostname|VARCHAR|_HOSTNAME
systemd_unit|VARCHAR|_SYSTEMD_UNIT
user_unit|VARCHAR|_SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT
identifier|VARCHAR|SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER
priority|INTEGER|PRIORITY
pid|BIGINT|_PID
uid|BIGINT|_UID
gid|BIGINT|_GID
boot_id|VARCHAR|_BOOT_ID
transport|VARCHAR|_TRANSPORT
cursor|VARCHAR|__CURSOR
entry|JSON|Complete Journal Entry
.TE
.PP
Stable columns use non-throwing conversion.
A missing, repeated, binary, or invalid stable source value becomes
.BR NULL .
The
.B entry
column contains the complete original Journal Entry as JSON.
It includes application-defined fields and values that cannot convert to a
stable column.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory in which to create the private Journal Selection file.
If you do not set this variable, or if you set it to an empty value,
.B journql
uses
.BR /tmp .
.TP
.B PATH
.B journql
uses this variable to find
.BR journalctl .
The package uses its private DuckDB executable and does not find DuckDB
through this variable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/bin/journql
The command.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/journql/duckdb
The command's private DuckDB executable.
.TP
.BI ${TMPDIR:\-/tmp}/journql. XXXXXXXXXX
A private, temporary Journal Selection file that contains newline-delimited
JSON.
.B journql
makes the file accessible only to the current user.
It removes the file after use and on
.BR HUP ,
.BR INT ,
or
.BR TERM .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The Journal Query completed successfully, or
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The command could not create the private Journal Selection file.
.TP
.B 2
The command syntax or Journal Selection was not valid.
.TP
.B 127
.B journql
could not find
.B journalctl
or the private DuckDB executable.
.TP
.BR 129 , " " 130 , " " 143
The command received
.BR HUP ,
.BR INT ,
or
.BR TERM ,
respectively.
.PP
If
.B journalctl
or DuckDB fails,
.B journql
returns that command's nonzero status.
.SH LIMITS
.B journql
stores the complete Journal Selection as newline-delimited JSON in a temporary
file before DuckDB starts.
A large Journal Selection can use temporary disk space approximately equal to
its JSON size.
The DuckDB database is in memory.
If the system has limited disk or memory, select fewer Journal Entries.
.PP
This release sets no maximum number of Journal Entries, Journal Selection
size, or Journal Query size.
Practical limits come from
.BR journalctl ,
the temporary file system, available memory, DuckDB, and the operating system.
.PP
The command is read-only.
It does not write to the journal, provide a persistent cache, or infer SQL
columns for application-defined fields.
It does not support remote journals, journal maintenance, interactive paging,
or follow mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count the Journal Entries in the Journal Selection:
.EX
journql \-\- \-\- 'SELECT count(*) FROM journal;'
.EE
.PP
Select entries for one systemd unit:
.EX
journql \-\- \-\-unit sshd.service \-\- \\
  'SELECT timestamp, message FROM journal ORDER BY timestamp;'
.EE
.PP
Write a JSON Query Result for recent Journal Entries:
.EX
journql \-\-format json \-\- \-\-since '1 hour ago' \-\- \\
  'SELECT priority, count(*) AS count FROM journal GROUP BY priority;'
.EE
.PP
Read an application-defined field from the complete Journal Entry:
.EX
journql \-\- \-\- \\
  "SELECT entry\->>'REQUEST_ID' AS request_id FROM journal;"
.EE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd.journal\-fields (7)
